+# Build loading and unloading
+
+## The problem
+
+When a player with a big saved build joined, the server froze for about 2 seconds. A server
+MicroProfiler capture showed one 2,100 ms frame, almost all of it under `Script_Builds`, and every
+player rubber-banded. Right after that, clients hitched in `Replicator ProcessPackets`. Players
+leaving with big builds caused a smaller freeze.
+
+The cause was in `src/ServerScriptService/Builds.server.luau`:
+
+- **Everything happened in one frame.** `loadBuild` created every saved block in one loop, and
+  saves go up to 20,000 blocks. `onPlayerRemoving` read every block and destroyed the whole plot
+  in the same frame too.
+- **Each block was three instances.** Each block was a `Part` plus an `Owner` StringValue and a
+  `PlacedAt` NumberValue, so a 20,000-block build meant 60,000 instances to create, replicate and
+  destroy.
+- **Parts were parented first.** `Instance.new("Part", parent)` put the part in the world before
+  its properties were set, so the engine handled each part while it was still being set up.
+- **Clients got everything at once.** All 60,000 new instances went out together, which caused
+  the `ProcessPackets` hitch on every client.
+
+## What changed
+
+All changes are in `Builds.server.luau`.
+
+1. **Loading and unloading are spread over many frames.** Joining queues a *load job*; leaving
+   queues an *unload job*. A `Heartbeat` worker moves the queued jobs forward each frame, with a
+   shared limit of **4 ms** and **500 blocks** per frame (`FRAME_BUDGET`,
+   `MAX_BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`). The limit covers all jobs together, so 8 players joining at once costs
+   no more per frame than one. The block cap also limits how much new replication data each frame
+   creates, so clients get a build as a steady stream instead of one burst.
+2. **One instance per block.** The placed time is now a `PlacedAt` **attribute** on the part
+   (`part:GetAttribute("PlacedAt")`, Unix seconds, the same value as before). The `Owner` value is
+   gone. The server tracks ownership itself: each plot keeps a `blocks` table that maps each part
+   to its cell.
+3. **Parts are cloned from templates and parented last.** There is one fully set-up template part
+   per block type. Each block is a `Clone()` with its `CFrame` and attribute set before it gets a
+   `Parent`, so it enters the world and replicates once, fully set up.
+4. **Unloading uses the server's own block list.** It no longer calls `GetChildren()` or reads
+   `Position` on every part. It frees each cell in `occupied` using the exact cell key it stored,
+   destroys parts a batch at a time, and destroys the folder once it is empty.
+
+### Placing and removing blocks
+
+- **Placing** works as before: same checks (type, NaN, rate limit, `Y >= 0`, free cell, reach),
+  and the block gets `PlacedAt = os.time()`.
+- **Removing** still lets players remove only their own blocks. The check is now "this part is in
+  the player's own plot's `blocks` table" instead of reading the `Owner` StringValue. Blocks only
+  ever go into their owner's plot, so this allows and rejects the same blocks as before. It is also
+  stricter: a client can't get past it with a crafted instance, and nobody can remove blocks from
+  a plot that is unloading.
+
+### Edge cases
+
+- **Leaving mid-load:** the plot is marked closed, the load stops at its next step, and the unload
+  job removes whatever blocks were already created.
+- **Plot slots:** a slot stays taken until its old plot is fully unloaded, so a new build never
+  loads into cells the old one still holds. If all 8 slots are busy because a leaving player's plot
+  is still unloading, the joining player **waits for a free slot** (and a warning is logged)
+  instead of getting no plot at all, which is what happened before.
+- **`SavedBuilds.load` is still called synchronously.** It builds the 20,000-entry test table in
+  one go, which is pure Lua work and small next to the instance work. Once saves come from a
+  DataStore this call will yield, and the code already handles a player leaving during that wait.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **No more server-wide freeze or rubber-banding** when someone with a big build joins or leaves.
+- **Builds appear over about a second instead of all at once.** Saved blocks are stored in layer
+  order (`y` is the outer loop), so a build grows from the ground up. A rough estimate for a
+  20,000-block build: at most 500 blocks per frame means at least 40 frames (about 0.7 s at
+  60 Hz). On a slower server the 4 ms limit is reached first and it takes longer, perhaps 1 to 3
+  seconds. If several big builds load together, they load one after another, oldest first, so the
+  last one can take several seconds to appear. Tune with `FRAME_BUDGET` and
+  `MAX_BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`.
+- **A leaving player's build disappears over a similar short time** instead of vanishing at once.
+- **During loading, the owner can already place blocks.** If they place one in a cell their saved
+  build hasn't filled yet, that saved block is skipped, the same rule as before (a saved block
+  never overwrites an occupied cell).
+- **Lighter client hitch.** Clients get a third of the instances, spread over many frames, so the
+  `Replicator ProcessPackets` spike should mostly go away. Clients still have to download and
+  render 20,000 parts per big build.
+
+## For upcoming work
+
+- **Placed-time feature:** read `block:GetAttribute("PlacedAt")`. It works on the server and on
+  clients, because attributes replicate. `PlacedAt` NumberValue children no longer exist.
+- **Anything that read `Owner`:** nothing in the project did. On the server, use the plot's
+  `blocks` table. A client can tell who owns a block from its plot folder's name, which is the
+  owner's UserId.
+- **Still worth doing:** turning on `Workspace.StreamingEnabled` would mean clients only receive
+  plots near them, which matters with 8 × 20,000 parts. That changes gameplay (far-away builds
+  aren't loaded on the client), so it is left as a separate decision.
+
+## Not yet verified
+
+These changes pass `stylua`, `selene` and `luau-lsp analyze`, but they haven't been profiled in a
+live server. To confirm the fix, capture the server MicroProfiler while a 20,000-block player joins
+and leaves. `Script_Builds` should stay at about 4 ms per frame for the whole load, with no long
+frame. Then check a client's `Replicator ProcessPackets` at the same moment.
